<title>USA: Federal officials lobby Massachusetts on Burma.</title>
<headline>Federal officials lobby Massachusetts on Burma.</headline>
<dateline>BOSTON 1997-04-15</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Union has asked the Clinton administration to convince Massachusetts to repeal a law that bars the state from doing business with firms that do business with Burma, a legislator said on Tuesday.</p>
<p>Massachusetts Rep. Byron Rushing, D-Boston, who sponsored the state legislation that went into effect January 1, met officials from the State Department and the U.S. trade representative's office for what he described as &quot;an informational meeting.&quot;</p>
<p>The EU has written a second letter to Washington demanding that the Clinton Administration take action within four weeks, Rushing said.</p>
<p>The federal officials also met with representatives from the governor's office and the attorney general's office.</p>
<p>The law has created an international furor among U.S. trading partners. It affects more than 500 companies including: Bayerische Motoren-Werke AG (BMW), Bank of Tokyo Ltd, Mitsubishi Corp and Siemens AG.</p>
</text>
